{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791636003,"nonce":"zkaT/UDEl9qEE1G8kvbHF/Zx/hk=","message":"The two window-sized targets in Canvas2D are different things. One is the frame layer: with a backdrop in the list, Renderer.draw draws the whole frame into a texture, since a window surface cannot be read, and blits it at the end. It is window-sized by nature. The other is the backdrop-rows intermediate (reserveRows), which only needs the backdrop's rect plus the blur's reach: bounding it saves one window-sized texture, about 12.7 MB here, with an origin offset in BackdropRowsShader and BackdropShader. Dropping the frame layer as well would need the surface configured with COPY_SRC, through hlwindow and xgpu, so the region under a backdrop can be copied out of it.","files":null}]}